 "researcher_notes": "Not on the roster under any name (searched Islander and Long Island Sound). Islander East Pipeline Company was owned equally by Duke Energy and KeySpan subsidiaries. It filed with FERC in June 2001 for about 50 miles of new 24-inch pipe, including about 22 miles on the floor of Long Island Sound, from a connection with Algonquin in New Haven, Connecticut to Suffolk County near Yaphank, New York. FERC issued the certificate in September 2002. Capacity is 260,000 dekatherms a day in the certificate story; Natural Gas Intelligence gave 285,000 in 2005 and up to 400 million cubic feet a day later. Cost of $180 million is from the 2005 Natural Gas Intelligence story. A federal court set aside the Commerce Department decision in 2005 and the project was described as blocked in 2007. No source found that states formal withdrawal, so cancelled is inferred from the long block and no construction. The Natural Gas Intelligence stories load only through the fetch ladder. Owners: entity lookup found Duke Energy as an exact existing entity; KeySpan has no existing entity and no legal form was found, so it is recorded as the source spells it. Ownership shares are 50 percent each. No route found; no official GIS located. Length is above the 25 km add threshold; threshold steps met are sponsor, endpoints and FERC certificate."
